1
00:00:05,398 --> 00:00:10,597
As I had grown accustomed to my expectations, I had insensibly begun to notice their effect

2
00:00:10,597 --> 00:00:16,597
upon myself and those around me. Their influence on my own character I disguised

3
00:00:16,597 --> 00:00:23,641
from my recognition as much as possible, but I knew very well that it was not all good. I

4
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:30,160
lived in a state of chronic uneasiness respecting my behaviour to Joe. My conscience was not by any

5
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:37,199
means comfortable about Biddy. When I woke up in the night,—like Camilla,—I used to think,

6
00:00:37,199 --> 00:00:41,038
with a weariness on my spirits, that I should have been happier and better if I

7
00:00:41,038 --> 00:00:46,199
had never seen Miss Havisham’s face, and had risen to manhood content to be partners with

8
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:52,678
Joe in the honest old forge. Many a time of an evening, when I sat alone looking at the fire,

9
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:58,597
I thought, after all there was no fire like the forge fire and the kitchen fire at home.

10
00:00:58,597 --> 00:01:03,917
Yet Estella was so inseparable from all my restlessness and disquiet of mind, that I really

11
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:10,000
fell into confusion as to the limits of my own part in its production. That is to say, supposing

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,800
I had had no expectations, and yet had had Estella to think of, I could not make out to my

13
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:21,917
satisfaction that I should have done much better. Now, concerning the influence of my position on

14
00:01:21,917 --> 00:01:28,237
others, I was in no such difficulty, and so I perceived—though dimly enough perhaps—that it was

15
00:01:28,237 --> 00:01:36,038
not beneficial to anybody, and, above all, that it was not beneficial to Herbert. My lavish habits

16
00:01:36,038 --> 00:01:42,718
led his easy nature into expenses that he could not afford, corrupted the simplicity of his life,

17
00:01:42,718 --> 00:01:49,078
and disturbed his peace with anxieties and regrets. I was not at all remorseful for

18
00:01:49,078 --> 00:01:55,078
having unwittingly set those other branches of the Pocket family to the poor arts they practised;

19
00:01:55,078 --> 00:02:00,398
because such littlenesses were their natural bent, and would have been evoked by anybody else,

20
00:02:00,398 --> 00:02:06,597
if I had left them slumbering. But Herbert’s was a very different case, and it often caused me a

21
00:02:06,597 --> 00:02:12,038
twinge to think that I had done him evil service in crowding his sparely furnished chambers with

22
00:02:12,038 --> 00:02:18,277
incongruous upholstery work, and placing the Canary-breasted Avenger at his disposal.

23
00:02:18,277 --> 00:02:24,000
So now, as an infallible way of making little ease great ease, I began to contract

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,917
a quantity of debt. I could hardly begin but Herbert must begin too, so he soon followed.

25
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:37,358
At Startop’s suggestion, we put ourselves down for election into a club called The Finches of

26
00:02:37,358 --> 00:02:42,237
the Grove: the object of which institution I have never divined, if it were not that the

27
00:02:42,237 --> 00:02:47,358
members should dine expensively once a fortnight, to quarrel among themselves as much as possible

28
00:02:47,358 --> 00:02:54,116
after dinner, and to cause six waiters to get drunk on the stairs. I know that these gratifying

29
00:02:54,116 --> 00:02:59,237
social ends were so invariably accomplished, that Herbert and I understood nothing else

30
00:02:59,237 --> 00:03:05,199
to be referred to in the first standing toast of the society: which ran “Gentlemen,

31
00:03:05,199 --> 00:03:12,436
may the present promotion of good feeling ever reign predominant among the Finches of the Grove.”

32
00:03:12,438 --> 00:03:17,680
The Finches spent their money foolishly (the Hotel we dined at was in Covent Garden),

33
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:22,519
and the first Finch I saw when I had the honour of joining the Grove was Bentley Drummle,

34
00:03:22,519 --> 00:03:27,680
at that time floundering about town in a cab of his own, and doing a great deal of damage to the

35
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:33,559
posts at the street corners. Occasionally, he shot himself out of his equipage headforemost

36
00:03:33,559 --> 00:03:39,438
over the apron; and I saw him on one occasion deliver himself at the door of the Grove in

37
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:46,961
this unintentional way—like coals. But here I anticipate a little, for I was not a Finch,

38
00:03:46,961 --> 00:03:52,758
and could not be, according to the sacred laws of the society, until I came of age.

39
00:03:52,758 --> 00:03:58,758
In my confidence in my own resources, I would willingly have taken Herbert’s expenses on myself;

40
00:03:58,758 --> 00:04:05,199
but Herbert was proud, and I could make no such proposal to him. So he got into difficulties in

41
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:12,116
every direction, and continued to look about him. When we gradually fell into keeping late hours and

42
00:04:12,116 --> 00:04:17,358
late company, I noticed that he looked about him with a desponding eye at breakfast-time;

43
00:04:17,358 --> 00:04:22,237
that he began to look about him more hopefully about midday; that he drooped when he came into

44
00:04:22,237 --> 00:04:28,398
dinner; that he seemed to descry Capital in the distance, rather clearly, after dinner;

45
00:04:28,398 --> 00:04:34,116
that he all but realised Capital towards midnight; and that at about two o’clock in the morning,

46
00:04:34,116 --> 00:04:39,680
he became so deeply despondent again as to talk of buying a rifle and going to America,

47
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:44,917
with a general purpose of compelling buffaloes to make his fortune.

48
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:50,597
I was usually at Hammersmith about half the week, and when I was at Hammersmith I haunted Richmond,

49
00:04:50,597 --> 00:04:57,800
whereof separately by and by. Herbert would often come to Hammersmith when I was there, and I think

50
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:02,277
at those seasons his father would occasionally have some passing perception that the opening he

51
00:05:02,277 --> 00:05:08,961
was looking for, had not appeared yet. But in the general tumbling up of the family, his tumbling

52
00:05:08,961 --> 00:05:16,398
out in life somewhere, was a thing to transact itself somehow. In the meantime Mr. Pocket grew

53
00:05:16,398 --> 00:05:23,557
greyer, and tried oftener to lift himself out of his perplexities by the hair. While Mrs. Pocket

54
00:05:23,559 --> 00:05:30,038
tripped up the family with her footstool, read her book of dignities, lost her pocket-handkerchief,

55
00:05:30,038 --> 00:05:35,718
told us about her grandpapa, and taught the young idea how to shoot, by shooting it into

56
00:05:35,718 --> 00:05:41,758
bed whenever it attracted her notice. As I am now generalising a period of

57
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:47,199
my life with the object of clearing my way before me, I can scarcely do so better than

58
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:52,557
by at once completing the description of our usual manners and customs at Barnard’s Inn.

59
00:05:52,559 --> 00:05:56,358
We spent as much money as we could, and got as little for it as people

60
00:05:56,358 --> 00:06:00,639
could make up their minds to give us. We were always more or less miserable,

61
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:06,480
and most of our acquaintance were in the same condition. There was a gay fiction

62
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:11,959
among us that we were constantly enjoying ourselves, and a skeleton truth that we

63
00:06:11,961 --> 00:06:20,398
never did. To the best of my belief, our case was in the last aspect a rather common one.

64
00:06:20,398 --> 00:06:26,436
Every morning, with an air ever new, Herbert went into the City to look about him. I often

65
00:06:26,438 --> 00:06:33,519
paid him a visit in the dark back-room in which he consorted with an ink-jar, a hat-peg, a coal-box,

66
00:06:33,519 --> 00:06:41,199
a string-box, an almanac, a desk and stool, and a ruler; and I do not remember that I ever saw him

67
00:06:41,199 --> 00:06:47,199
do anything else but look about him. If we all did what we undertake to do, as faithfully as

68
00:06:47,199 --> 00:06:54,078
Herbert did, we might live in a Republic of the Virtues. He had nothing else to do, poor fellow,

69
00:06:54,078 --> 00:07:00,398
except at a certain hour of every afternoon to “go to Lloyd’s”—in observance of a ceremony of seeing

70
00:07:00,398 --> 00:07:06,678
his principal, I think. He never did anything else in connection with Lloyd’s that I could find out,

71
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:12,160
except come back again. When he felt his case unusually serious, and that

72
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:19,160
he positively must find an opening, he would go on ’Change at a busy time, and walk in and out,

73
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:26,917
in a kind of gloomy country dance figure, among the assembled magnates. “For,” says Herbert to me,

74
00:07:26,917 --> 00:07:32,319
coming home to dinner on one of those special occasions, “I find the truth to be, Handel,

75
00:07:32,319 --> 00:07:38,678
that an opening won’t come to one, but one must go to it,—so I have been.”

76
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:42,961
If we had been less attached to one another, I think we must have hated one another regularly

77
00:07:42,961 --> 00:07:48,641
every morning. I detested the chambers beyond expression at that period of repentance,

78
00:07:48,641 --> 00:07:54,078
and could not endure the sight of the Avenger’s livery; which had a more expensive and a less

79
00:07:54,078 --> 00:08:00,680
remunerative appearance then than at any other time in the four-and-twenty hours. As we got more

80
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:05,237
and more into debt, breakfast became a hollower and hollower form, and,

81
00:08:05,237 --> 00:08:10,879
being on one occasion at breakfast-time threatened (by letter) with legal proceedings,

82
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:17,480
“not unwholly unconnected,” as my local paper might put it, “with jewelery,” I went so far

83
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:22,718
as to seize the Avenger by his blue collar and shake him off his feet,—so that he was

84
00:08:22,718 --> 00:08:30,519
actually in the air, like a booted Cupid,—for presuming to suppose that we wanted a roll.

85
00:08:30,519 --> 00:08:33,038
At certain times—meaning at uncertain times,

86
00:08:33,038 --> 00:08:40,237
for they depended on our humour—I would say to Herbert, as if it were a remarkable discovery,—

87
00:08:40,237 --> 00:08:43,436
“My dear Herbert, we are getting on badly.”

88
00:08:43,438 --> 00:08:46,917
“My dear Handel,” Herbert would say to me, in all sincerity,

89
00:08:46,917 --> 00:08:52,078
“if you will believe me, those very words were on my lips, by a strange coincidence.”

90
00:08:52,078 --> 00:08:57,078
“Then, Herbert,” I would respond, “let us look into our affairs.”

91
00:08:57,078 --> 00:09:02,641
We always derived profound satisfaction from making an appointment for this purpose. I

92
00:09:02,641 --> 00:09:06,277
always thought this was business, this was the way to confront the thing,

93
00:09:06,277 --> 00:09:12,398
this was the way to take the foe by the throat. And I know Herbert thought so too.

94
00:09:12,398 --> 00:09:14,597
We ordered something rather special for dinner,

95
00:09:14,597 --> 00:09:18,839
with a bottle of something similarly out of the common way, in order that our minds

96
00:09:18,839 --> 00:09:25,480
might be fortified for the occasion, and we might come well up to the mark. Dinner over,

97
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:30,639
we produced a bundle of pens, a copious supply of ink, and a goodly show of writing

98
00:09:30,641 --> 00:09:36,358
and blotting paper. For there was something very comfortable in having plenty of stationery.

99
00:09:36,358 --> 00:09:41,319
I would then take a sheet of paper, and write across the top of it, in a neat hand,

100
00:09:41,319 --> 00:09:48,480
the heading, “Memorandum of Pip’s debts”; with Barnard’s Inn and the date very carefully added.

101
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:53,358
Herbert would also take a sheet of paper, and write across it with similar formalities,

102
00:09:53,358 --> 00:09:58,597
“Memorandum of Herbert’s debts.” Each of us would then refer to a

103
00:09:58,597 --> 00:10:03,319
confused heap of papers at his side, which had been thrown into drawers,

104
00:10:03,319 --> 00:10:10,078
worn into holes in pockets, half burnt in lighting candles, stuck for weeks into the looking-glass,

105
00:10:10,078 --> 00:10:17,358
and otherwise damaged. The sound of our pens going refreshed us exceedingly, insomuch that

106
00:10:17,358 --> 00:10:22,480
I sometimes found it difficult to distinguish between this edifying business proceeding and

107
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:29,959
actually paying the money. In point of meritorious character, the two things seemed about equal.

108
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:35,438
When we had written a little while, I would ask Herbert how he got on? Herbert probably would

109
00:10:35,438 --> 00:10:41,237
have been scratching his head in a most rueful manner at the sight of his accumulating figures.

110
00:10:41,237 --> 00:10:46,718
“They are mounting up, Handel,” Herbert would say; “upon my life, they are mounting up.”

111
00:10:46,718 --> 00:10:50,680
“Be firm, Herbert,” I would retort, plying my own pen with

112
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:59,016
great assiduity. “Look the thing in the face. Look into your affairs. Stare them out of countenance.”

113
00:10:59,016 --> 00:11:00,877
“So I would, Handel,

114
00:11:00,879 --> 00:11:04,718
only they are staring me out of countenance.”

115
00:11:04,718 --> 00:11:07,758
However, my determined manner would have its effect,

116
00:11:07,758 --> 00:11:13,078
and Herbert would fall to work again. After a time he would give up once more,

117
00:11:13,078 --> 00:11:20,480
on the plea that he had not got Cobbs’s bill, or Lobbs’s, or Nobbs’s, as the case might be.

118
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:26,277
“Then, Herbert, estimate; estimate it in round numbers, and put it down.”

119
00:11:26,277 --> 00:11:29,800
“What a fellow of resource you are!” my friend would reply,

120
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:34,641
with admiration. “Really your business powers are very remarkable.”

121
00:11:34,641 --> 00:11:39,078
I thought so too. I established with myself, on these occasions, the reputation

122
00:11:39,078 --> 00:11:46,000
of a first-rate man of business,—prompt, decisive, energetic, clear, cool-headed.

123
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,917
When I had got all my responsibilities down upon my list, I compared each with the bill,

124
00:11:50,917 --> 00:11:58,839
and ticked it off. My self-approval when I ticked an entry was quite a luxurious sensation. When

125
00:11:58,839 --> 00:12:04,718
I had no more ticks to make, I folded all my bills up uniformly, docketed each on the back,

126
00:12:04,718 --> 00:12:10,160
and tied the whole into a symmetrical bundle. Then I did the same for Herbert (who modestly said he

127
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:16,597
had not my administrative genius), and felt that I had brought his affairs into a focus for him.

128
00:12:16,597 --> 00:12:23,879
My business habits had one other bright feature, which I called “leaving a Margin.” For example;

129
00:12:23,879 --> 00:12:28,199
supposing Herbert’s debts to be one hundred and sixty-four pounds four-and-twopence,

130
00:12:28,199 --> 00:12:33,358
I would say, “Leave a margin, and put them down at two hundred.” Or,

131
00:12:33,358 --> 00:12:38,078
supposing my own to be four times as much, I would leave a margin, and put them down

132
00:12:38,078 --> 00:12:43,917
at seven hundred. I had the highest opinion of the wisdom of this same Margin, but I am

133
00:12:43,917 --> 00:12:49,758
bound to acknowledge that on looking back, I deem it to have been an expensive device. For,

134
00:12:49,758 --> 00:12:54,961
we always ran into new debt immediately, to the full extent of the margin, and sometimes,

135
00:12:54,961 --> 00:13:00,237
in the sense of freedom and solvency it imparted, got pretty far on into another margin.

136
00:13:01,917 --> 00:13:09,559
But there was a calm, a rest, a virtuous hush, consequent on these examinations of our affairs

137
00:13:09,559 --> 00:13:17,116
that gave me, for the time, an admirable opinion of myself. Soothed by my exertions, my method,

138
00:13:17,116 --> 00:13:21,879
and Herbert’s compliments, I would sit with his symmetrical bundle and my own on the table before

139
00:13:21,879 --> 00:13:28,319
me among the stationery, and feel like a Bank of some sort, rather than a private individual.

140
00:13:28,319 --> 00:13:31,237
We shut our outer door on these solemn occasions,

141
00:13:31,237 --> 00:13:36,961
in order that we might not be interrupted. I had fallen into my serene state one evening,

142
00:13:36,961 --> 00:13:40,116
when we heard a letter dropped through the slit in the said door,

143
00:13:40,116 --> 00:13:47,718
and fall on the ground. “It’s for you, Handel,” said Herbert, going out and coming back with it,

144
00:13:47,718 --> 00:13:54,519
“and I hope there is nothing the matter.” This was in allusion to its heavy black seal and border.

145
00:13:54,519 --> 00:14:00,118
The letter was signed Trabb & Co., and its contents were simply, that I was an honoured sir,

146
00:14:00,718 --> 00:14:06,961
and that they begged to inform me that Mrs. J. Gargery had departed this life on Monday last at

147
00:14:06,961 --> 00:14:12,680
twenty minutes past six in the evening, and that my attendance was requested at the interment on

148
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:17,000
Monday next at three o’clock in the afternoon. Chapter 35.
